# Programming Principles

When generating stack rules, incorporate these principles adapted to the specific language/framework.

## Core Principles

PrincipleDefinitions {
  DOT = "Do One Thing - single responsibility per function"
  YAGNI = "You Aren't Gonna Need It - no premature abstraction"
  KISS = "Keep It Simple - prefer straightforward over clever"
  DRY = "Don't Repeat Yourself - extract after pattern stabilizes"
  SDA = "Self Describing APIs - names communicate intent"
}

### DOT - Do One Thing

(function does multiple things) => suggest splitting
(concern mixing: IO with logic, mapping with effects) => separate

generateRules() {
  Encourage small, focused functions
  Suggest splitting when functions grow
  Separate concerns
}

### YAGNI - You Aren't Gonna Need It

(premature abstraction detected) => push back
(over-engineering "what if") => suggest simpler solution

LifecycleCalibration {
  PoC => maximum YAGNI
  MVP => high YAGNI
  Production => moderate YAGNI
}

### KISS - Keep It Simple

(clever code) => suggest readable alternative
(novel pattern) => prefer standard approach

### DRY - Don't Repeat Yourself

DRYThresholds {
  PoC => wait for patterns
  MVP => extract after 3x
  Production => extract after 2x
}

(incidental similarity) => leave as is
(true duplication: same concept) => extract

### SDA - Self Describing APIs

(generic names: data, payload, info) => suggest specific names
(positional arguments) => prefer named parameters/options

explicitDefaults() {
  """
  const createUser = ({ id = createId(), name = '' } = {}) => ({ id, name })
  """
}

## Style Principles

StylePreferences {
  Immutability = "const/final/readonly by default, spread over mutation"
  Composition = "combine simple pieces, avoid inheritance hierarchies"
  Declarative = "map/filter/reduce over loops, describe what not how"
}

(deep inheritance chain) => warn, suggest composition
(manual loop) => suggest declarative alternative

## Composition Philosophy

CompositionRules {
  ChainOverIntermediate => eliminate unnecessary variable names
  ConsistentPatterns => same idioms sync and async
  SimplestConstruct => if simpler syntax exists, use it
  PipelineStyle => data flows through transformations
}

(intermediate variable) => chain if no reuse
(inconsistent async pattern) => unify with sync equivalent
(complex construct) => simplify to simpler equivalent

## API Philosophy

APIRules {
  ExplicitDefaults => parameter defaults over runtime fallbacks
  DestructuredParams => named parameters over positional
  SelfDescribing => signature tells you types and defaults
  CohesionOverType => group by feature, not by technical role
}

(runtime fallback for default) => use parameter default instead
(positional args > 2) => use options object
(grouped by type) => group by feature instead

## Naming Conventions

NamingRules {
  Functions => verbs: calculate, fetch, create, update
  Predicates => questions: isEmpty, hasPermission, canEdit
  Negatives => strong form: isEmpty over !hasItems
  Redundancy => avoid: filter(users) not filterUsersList
  Lifecycle => beforeX/afterX over willX/didX
}

## Comment Guidelines

CommentRules {
  When => "why" not "what", non-obvious logic, workarounds
  Not => obvious code, restating code, every parameter
}

TeamCalibration {
  Solo => minimal, future-self only
  SmallTeam => why not what
  LargeTeam => public APIs
  OpenSource => comprehensive
}

## Lifecycle Calibration

| Principle | PoC | MVP | Production |
|-----------|-----|-----|------------|
| DOT | Relaxed | Encouraged | Required |
| YAGNI | Maximum | High | Moderate |
| KISS | Maximum | High | High |
| DRY | Wait | After 3x | After 2x |
| SDA | Basic | Good | Strict |
| Immutability | Optional | Preferred | Required for shared |
| Comments | Minimal | Why only | Why + APIs |
